    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Record growth, record lows: FPS drops 14% to just above its all-time low",
               "technical":  "FPS closed at $18.00 on 2026-11-20, down 14.12% on the day. It opened at $20.50 and traded as low as $17.75, just above the record low of $17.67 set on 2026-11-06 and 73% below the 52-week high of $66. The trend is clearly down. Price sits far below the EMA 50 ($28.32), EMA 100 ($32.00) and EMA 200 ($37.25). EMAs are moving averages that give more weight to recent prices, and these three are stacked in bearish order, with the short-term average below the long-term ones. Momentum is weak but has stopped getting worse: MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is -2.95 against its signal line at -3.00, leaving a barely positive histogram of +0.05. Friday\u0027s drop could easily turn it negative again. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 35.18, which is weak but not yet oversold. Price is close to the lower Bollinger Band at $16.85 (the 20-day middle band is $23.14), so the stock is stretched to the downside. Volume was 13.9M shares, about 1.3x the 20-day average of 10.7M, which shows real selling. Support: $17.67–17.75. Resistance: $23.14, then $28–29.",
               "fundamental":  "Forgent makes switchgear and power-distribution equipment that sits between the grid and the servers, so AI data-center construction feeds directly into its sales. On 2026-11-12 it reported fiscal Q1 2027 (quarter ended 30 Sep). Revenue was $478.6M, up 69% year on year, with net income of $54.8M (+252%) and adjusted EBITDA of $104.9M. Full-year guidance went up to $2.45–2.65B in revenue and $1.30–1.44 in adjusted EPS. Management says the backlog fully covers that guidance (backlog was a record $3B at fiscal year-end in June) and reports \u0027meaningful traction with multiple frontier AI labs and hyperscalers.\u0027 There were weak spots: gross margin slipped to 33.1% from 34.6%, adjusted EBITDA margin fell to 21.9% from 23.0%, and the new guidance midpoints were reportedly slightly below consensus. At $18, the stock trades at about 13x the midpoint of adjusted EPS guidance.",
               "news":  [
                            "2026-10-22: CFO Ryan Fiedler stepped down, citing family reasons, and moved into a consulting role. EVP of Finance Amy Ard took over. Shares fell more than 23% that day and dropped below the $27 IPO price.",
                            "2026-11-05 and 2026-11-16: AI power-equipment stocks sold off broadly on worries about hyperscaler spending and data-center policy (Vertiv fell 14% and 6.6% on those days). FPS fell with the group and hit a record low of $17.67 on 2026-11-06.",
                            "2026-11-09: Forgent said in advance that fiscal Q1 revenue and adjusted EBITDA would beat its earlier guidance, and the shares rebounded. They gained another 14%+ on 2026-11-11 alongside CoreWeave\u0027s strong results.",
                            "2026-11-12: Fiscal Q1 2027 revenue rose 69% to $478.6M and adjusted EPS of $0.24 beat the ~$0.20 consensus, with full-year guidance raised. Shares still fell about 8–11% intraday because gross margin shrank and the guidance increase was smaller than hoped.",
                            "Analysts cut price targets during the slide, including Wells Fargo ($44→$25, Equal-Weight), Bernstein ($48→$33) and KeyBanc ($60→$46). The exact dates could not be confirmed from available sources.",
                            "2026-11-20: Shares fell 14.12% to $18.00 on above-average volume. No company announcement explains the move. It looks like continued selling of AI-infrastructure stocks rather than new company news."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The share price is telling a different story from the earnings. Three things keep pressure on it. First, private-equity owner Neos Partners has already sold stock in three follow-on offerings since the February IPO (the July deal priced at $49), and more shares can still come to market. Second, the CFO left eight months after listing and margins are slipping, both while the company ramps capacity to deliver a $3B backlog. Third, the stock tracks AI-spending sentiment: data centers were 42% of fiscal 2025 revenue. A close below $17.67 would put the stock at new all-time lows with no earlier support to lean on.",
               "opportunity":  "If the company keeps delivering, the gap between results and share price is wide. Revenue grew 69%, net income grew 252%, guidance went up, and the backlog covers this fiscal year. At about 13x adjusted EPS guidance, the market is pricing in a lot of doubt for a company guiding to roughly 80% revenue growth. On the chart, price is near the lower Bollinger Band, RSI is 35 and the MACD histogram has just turned positive, conditions where selling often slows. Holding above $17.67 and then climbing back above the $23 middle band would be the first signs of a bottom forming.",
               "risks":  [
                             "More share sales by private-equity owner Neos Partners (dilution and selling pressure)",
                             "Execution risk after the CFO change, while factory capacity is being ramped",
                             "Gross margin falling (33.1% vs 34.6% a year ago)",
                             "Hyperscalers or AI labs cutting or delaying data-center spending",
                             "Very high volatility; a close below $17.67 means new record lows with no earlier support",
                             "Short public track record (listed February 2026), so the moat is still unproven"
